            "content": [
              {
                "text": "**Observing Grid Patterns**\n\nI'm now focusing on 10x10 grids filled with 2x2 blocks of 5s. The initial pattern that I am zeroing in on is the arrangement. The blocks of 5s are separated by lines of 0s, and I am noticing the positions of the blocks in the first training example. Rows 1-2, 4-5, and so on... I am using this to formulate a potential rule.\n\n\n**Analyzing Bounding Boxes**\n\nI've made a breakthrough. I realized the importance of the bounding box around the 5s blocks. Cells within this box that intersect with empty rows or columns (the 0 lines) become 2. Cells outside the box at the intersection become 1. I'm verifying this with training example 2, noting the row and column extents of 5s.\n\n\n**Refining Rule Discovery**\n\nI'm now solidifying the rule. The key is in the separator rows and columns. I'm taking a hard look at how these separators, the empty rows and columns, behave relative to the bounding box of the 5s. Train examples 2 and 3 confirm that values within the bounding box at separator row/col locations are 2, while the values outside are 1. The challenge is formalizing the rule for separator identification.\n\n\n",
                "type": "reasoning_text"
              }
            ],
            "encrypted_content": null,
            "status": "completed",
            "format": "google-gemini-v1"
          },
          {
            "id": "msg_tmp_oyuowwhy2n",
            "content": [
              {
                "annotations": [],
                "text": "An explanation of the transformation rule:\n\n1. Identify the bounding box containing all grey (`5`) blocks: `[min_r, max_r]` and `[min_c, max_c]`.\n2. Find all row indices strictly between `min_r` and `max_r` that contain no `5`s (\"empty separator rows\").\n3. Find all column indices strictly between `min_c` and `max_c` that contain no `5`s (\"empty separator columns\").\n4. For each empty separator row:\n   - Cells inside the bounding box range `[min_c, max_c]` become red (`2`).\n   - Cells extending outside the bounding box range become blue (`1`).\n5. For each empty separator column:\n   - Cells inside the bounding box range `[min_r, max_r]` become red (`2`).\n   - Cells extending outside the bounding box range become blue (`1`).\n6. All other cells remain unchanged.",
